WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:19.919
I remember looking at the clouds while lying on the grass at our family farm near Cherkessk, a city in the Caucasus Mountains in the Soviet Union.

2
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:27.114
I'd lie there between my regular chores, and my father would say, "Alexei Samedov, you are a lazy boy."

3
00:00:27.115 --> 00:00:30.512
But I wasn't lazy; I was planning my future.

4
00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:38.476
I knew one day I would fly among the clouds, higher and higher—and maybe even reach the stars.

5
00:00:38.477 --> 00:00:42.710
I needed to study hard and get in good physical shape to be a pilot.

6
00:00:42.711 --> 00:00:50.459
So I made sure my grades were near perfect, and I ran fast around the boundary fence of our farm every day.

7
00:00:50.460 --> 00:01:00.097
In the Soviet Union, our compulsory education lasts 11 years, and after that I was accepted at the Sarapul Air Force Academy.

8
00:01:00.098 --> 00:01:07.643
For four years I learned about the physics of flight, airplane mechanics, aviation navigation, weather variability—

9
00:01:07.644 --> 00:01:11.668
everything I would need to become a successful pilot.

10
00:01:11.669 --> 00:01:19.111
And of course I actually flew planes—at first with an instructor and eventually solo.

11
00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:26.476
In 1947, when I was twenty years old, I attended the Borisoglebsk Military Aviation School,

12
00:01:26.477 --> 00:01:33.676
where I learned to fly the new Soviet fighter jets like the MiG-9 and the Yak-15.

13
00:01:33.677 --> 00:01:39.960
I was taught how to make tight turns and eject from my plane in case of trouble.

14
00:01:39.961 --> 00:01:47.498
After graduating I became a test pilot and flew one of the first MiG-15 fighter planes—

15
00:01:47.499 --> 00:01:52.460
the most technologically advanced fighter in the world at that time.

16
00:01:52.461 --> 00:01:59.783
After World War II, as rocket science developed, both the Soviet Union and the United States began a "space race,"

17
00:01:59.784 --> 00:02:03.712
an exciting competition to put humans into space.

18
00:02:03.713 --> 00:02:09.861
Both countries invested money and time into programs designed to pursue human spaceflight.

19
00:02:09.862 --> 00:02:18.208
The U.S. program was called Project Mercury, and the Soviet program was called the Vostok Program.

20
00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:28.275
In 1957 the Soviet Union put the first earthling into orbit around Earth—a dog named Laika—and the race really became serious.

21
00:02:28.276 --> 00:02:37.604
In 1959 the Cosmonaut Training Center was created in Moscow to teach Soviet pilots how to fly the new spacecraft and survive in space.

22
00:02:37.605 --> 00:02:45.043
The word "cosmonaut" means "sailor of the universe" in Russian, and I was one of the first to apply to become one.

23
00:02:45.044 --> 00:02:52.020
Thousands of pilots, engineers, and scientists—men and women—applied for the Soviet space program,

24
00:02:52.021 --> 00:02:56.793
and they had to pass extensive interviews with the director of the center.

25
00:02:56.794 --> 00:03:02.464
I also had to pass very strict medical examinations and psychological tests

26
00:03:02.465 --> 00:03:10.329
because it is extremely important the applicants be healthy and mentally able to withstand the stress and the loneliness of a space flight.

27
00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:16.843
Fewer than one out of every hundred people that applied were actually chosen, and luckily I made the cut.

28
00:03:16.844 --> 00:03:24.877
Our training includes working in many simulators, machines that mimic the environmental conditions a cosmonaut will experience in space.

29
00:03:24.878 --> 00:03:31.418
For example, I train in a pressurized space suit to adapt my body to various altitudes—

30
00:03:31.419 --> 00:03:37.383
first, I train at 5 to 6 kilometers and then, at 14 to 15 kilometers.

31
00:03:37.384 --> 00:03:47.426
When the spacecraft re-enters Earth's atmosphere, cosmonauts have to eject at about 12 kilometers of altitude and descend to Earth with a parachute.

32
00:03:47.427 --> 00:03:53.370
So I practice jumping from airplanes and landing with a parachute in water and on land.

33
00:03:53.371 --> 00:04:01.568
I also train with a gimbal rig, a machine that has taught me how to control the spin of a tumbling space capsule.

34
00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:09.213
My training in a centrifuge, a huge mechanical arm, helps me withstand spinning forward and backward,

35
00:04:09.214 --> 00:04:13.872
and round and round at differing speeds for up to 13 minutes.

36
00:04:13.873 --> 00:04:18.014
The centrifuge has accustomed my body to high g-forces,

37
00:04:18.015 --> 00:04:24.113
the forces of gravity a cosmonaut experiences during the acceleration and deceleration of space flight.

38
00:04:24.114 --> 00:04:29.183
Roller-coaster riders also experience g-forces, but at a much lower level.

39
00:04:29.184 --> 00:04:34.507
The part of the training I like best is learning how to use the control panels in the capsule.

40
00:04:34.508 --> 00:04:38.913
I've learned how to maintain communication with the space team back on Earth,

41
00:04:38.914 --> 00:04:45.625
memorized every part of my spacecraft, and practiced what to do in case of an emergency.

42
00:04:45.626 --> 00:04:53.734
The worst part of the training is being left totally alone for very long periods in an isolation chamber, sometimes up to ten days.

43
00:04:53.735 --> 00:05:00.877
My instructors need to test my reactions to being alone and to the lack of outside stimulation.

44
00:05:00.878 --> 00:05:05.508
I call it the cabin of silence, and being in there is really difficult.

45
00:05:05.509 --> 00:05:09.346
At times I don't know if I am awake or asleep.

46
00:05:09.347 --> 00:05:16.305
Sometimes I pound on the walls of the chamber, sing, or talk to myself, just to make sure I can still feel and hear.

47
00:05:16.306 --> 00:05:19.580
While in isolation I think about a lot of things.

48
00:05:19.581 --> 00:05:25.074
I ask myself, "What will happen to my wife and children if I do not come back from a mission?

49
00:05:25.075 --> 00:05:30.625
Should I take this risk?" These are hard questions to ponder.

50
00:05:30.626 --> 00:05:36.570
Recently Yuri Gagarin became the first man in history to go up into space.

51
00:05:36.571 --> 00:05:46.012
On April 12, 1961, he spent 108 minutes in space and came back safely. Everyone in the world was amazed!

52
00:05:46.013 --> 00:05:55.554
Quickly the Americans launched astronauts Alan Shepard in May and Gus Grissom in July; each mission was a mere 15-minute suborbital flight.

53
00:05:55.555 --> 00:06:05.238
That August cosmonaut Gherman Titov bested the Americans when he took off and spent more than a day in space, circling Earth 17 times.

54
00:06:05.239 --> 00:06:08.876
He was the first human ever to fall asleep in space!

55
00:06:08.877 --> 00:06:17.091
Now I am waiting to hear, along with my fellow cosmonauts, who will be chosen to go on the next mission. I hope it will be me.

56
00:06:17.092 --> 00:06:23.992
My father is proud and so is my wife, but they worry too because space is a very dangerous place.

57
00:06:23.993 --> 00:06:31.471
But the galaxy is the last frontier and calls out to us on Earth to explore and discover what the universe holds for humanity.

58
00:06:31.472 --> 00:06:44.528
Hopefully I will fly there, just as I dreamed so long ago lying on the grass at the farm and looking up at the stars.
